{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789809210,"nonce":"b2FnweHI/IqIicKPWkDBm/ssz0Q=","title":"Late Cranelift OSR entries are compiled on the mutator","message":"late_osr_entry builds a Cranelift entry synchronously on the interpreter thread when a header turns hot after its function was compiled, on the reasoning that one costs ~1ms. deltablue builds 17 of them (ASH_PROFILE=phases: main / cranelift compile, 17 calls, 5.7-7.0ms) in a run of 46-65ms, so 9-12% of the row is compile time on the mutator. Each is short, but there are many, and every one stalls the loop it is meant to speed up.\n\nSubmitting the entry to the tier-0 broker and taking it at a later probe would move the compile off the mutator; the LLVM path already installs entries from the promoter thread, so the concurrent install is the mechanism that exists. The cost is that a loop which ends before the entry lands paid for a compile it never used, which today is paid on the mutator anyway.\n\nMeasured on the NUC pinned to physical cores (taskset -c 0,2,4,6); on 0-3 the compile threads share the mutator's hyperthreads and the picture is worse (see 85cd9fc).","files":null}]}